      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet because the dose looks like a probable error.\n\n**The problem:** Amlodipine 50 mg once daily is 5 times the maximum recommended dose. The usual adult range is 2.5\u201310 mg once daily, and 10 mg is the maximum. At 50 mg, the patient could develop severe low blood pressure, reflex tachycardia and shock. Amlodipine's long half-life (about 30\u201350 hours) would make that hard to reverse. Nothing in the patient's details (adult, 70 kg, normal kidney and liver function) suggests a dose above the standard range.\n\n**Possible intended doses:**\n- **5 mg once daily:** the usual starting dose. A slipped zero (5 \u2192 50) is a common transcription error.\n- **10 mg once daily:** the maximum dose, if you're titrating up.\n- **2.5 mg once daily:** sometimes used for elderly or frail patients, or when adding to other blood pressure medications.\n\nPlease confirm the dose you want and I'll enter it right away. If you do mean 50 mg, tell me the clinical reason and I'll add it as a note on the order. I'd also recommend checking with pharmacy, since a dose this far above the labeled maximum will probably be flagged."
      }
